\f1\fs22 When Sarah felt Chanta\'92s wounds were settled enough, she decided his looks would benefit from serious exfoliation. One night, she sat him on the edge of the bath, naked except for his boxer shorts, and produced a purple-black cloth to buff his head.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ow!\'94 Chanta complained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Men are such babies,\'94 Sarah scolded. \'93Look, it\'92s working.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta checked out his noggin in the mirror and was genuinely pleased. He ran his hand over his scalp, enjoying the smooth feel where Sarah had worked. He submitted to her resumption. \'93What is that cloth, anyway?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Giraffe tongue.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta jerked away. \'93What the actual tax? Aren\'92t they extinct?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, they are now,\'94 Sarah said, rubbing behind his ears.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Great. You\'92re a giraffe exterminator. Is there anything you Airborne won\'92t kill?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She shrugged. \'93I didn\'92t kill it. My grandmother left it to me when I was a child. Great sentimental value.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Really?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah. She used to polish her bowling balls with it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Sarah finished, Chanta proudly paraded for her. She applauded. He actually felt\'85new.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hey, I\'92m gonna take a shower. What\'92s say we go out for a couple of drinks? I\'92m dying to splash-on some of that new vodka you got me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah gave him a sideways look. \'93Are you asking me on a date?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta blushed and shrugged. \'93Course not, just some karaoke, you know\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How about at an Elvi bar?\'94 Sarah mischievously asked, believing she was upping the ante.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Of course.\'94 Chanta snorted. \'93What other kind is there?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah beamed as fully as if he\'92d invited her on a riverboat casino cruise for which neither of them would dress appropriately.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Later, they tumbled back into Chanta\'92s flat, both still hoarse from karaoke and laughter. Sarah wrangled Chanta\'92s arm around her shoulders and guided him past suspicious bulges in the carpet and the rogue plunger on his kitchenette floor. Every time their hands touched, Chanta\'92s heart tried to make a break for his windpipe.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah snatched a pair of his boxer shorts off the coffee table, folded them and lobbed them towards the laundry door.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They orbited each other, half-laughing at nothing at all, until the universe paused. Chanta, searching for wit, produced, \'93Do we ever get to see your place?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The moment unspooled. Sarah blinked, brushed imaginary crumbs from the bench, and swept a glance through the unlit corners. \'93I like it here. Feels like\'85 real life.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You sound like my landlord. \'91Intimate.\'92 \'91Quaint.\'92\'94 Chanta stared and waited for Sarah to admit hidden motivations. When she said nothing, he voiced his fear:\'93Are you just embarrassed to take a Cloudborne to your Airborne penthouse?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She stammered, \'93Why would I be embarrassed? I like the domestic stuff. Folding your shirts. Scrubbing decades of gunk off your mysterious brickwork. Polishing a stasis pantry older than Federation.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah. You don\'92t have to fake it. We both know your place floats so high you could take phone calls from satellites.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah straightened, adopting her best landlord-inspection voice. \'93Mr Chantara, have you been neglecting your shower grout again? I\'92m afraid that\'92s a breach of lease clause 4.2: \'91grubbiness above the permitted average.\'92\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s face collapsed in horror. \'93Don\'92t call me that.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why, Mr Anthony Chantara?\'94 Her pronunciation was impeccable, and she grinned at the effect, entirely too pleased with herself.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta recoiled as if slapped with a wet echidna. \'93Don\'92t\'85 look, just called me Chanta, okay? At least that doesn\'92t sound\'85 foreign.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah cocked an eyebrow, settling herself on his one clear table. \'93Do you actually know what it means?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta was deep into a theatrical eye roll. \'93Yeah, it means my\'85 parents\'85 no, no one explained my name to me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s Thai, Tony\'94 Sarah said, her tone softening. It means \'91moon water\'92. Moonlight on water. It means \'91brilliant\'92 or \'91to shine.\'92\'94 She skipped the part where Chantara is most commonly a feminine given name.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta scratched his scalp, knocked over a jar of teaspoons, and muttered, \'93Everyone at school just made fun of me. Even the teachers. No one at St Reinhart\'92s Public thought to look it up.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> She reached out and touched his freshly exfoliated head. \'93You\'92re a rare vintage, Mr Shiny-Brillant-Moonwater. Mr Chantara.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta shrugged and looked the floor.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92re shiny and brilliant, Tony\'94 Sarah told him, but Chanta seemed to recede and farther within himself. Sarah became impatient that he would not meet her gaze, so hers cast about the apartment. She became frustrated at the shabbiness of the place, in spite of her vigilant mission to make it liveable. \'93How can a cleaner live in such squalor?\'94 she blurted, making it sound funnier and more ironic than she genuinely felt. Spending time among the Cloudborne was wearing thin, less adventure and more voyeuristic tourism with each passing day.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s an art form,\'94 Chanta said. He finally met her eyes, as if he recognised a deeper truth in her words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The tension frayed, then snapped. Sarah scooped up her bag and paused by the door, one hand on the knob.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta made himself grin. \'93You, uh, running off?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah pretended to study her device. \'93Please. I just\'85 Look, Chanta, you\'92re\'97\'94 She stopped, reset with a practiced laugh. \'93You know, if I had any sense, I\'92d let you talk me into it. But apparently, I haven\'92t got that tonight.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta tried for levity: \'93Was it the serenade at the bar? Stark raving romance is a known hazard around Elvi.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah snorted\'97genuine, but not quite at the joke. For a moment, there was something unspoken, raw and wary, glimmering between them. \'93It\'92s not you. Well, it is\'97you\'92re infuriatingly\'85\'94 She couldn\'92t find the right adjective.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He shrugged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She smiled, awkward now. \'93I should go. Early shift. Conspiracies to unravel. Lives to almost save.\'94 She lingered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta wanted to ask, but didn\'92t. He watched her, wanting to close the space, but respectful of the alarm behind her eyes. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the door, she turned back, all bravado: \'93Goodnight, Mr Fairy Boxers.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He nodded and tried a smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah left, closing the door gently behind her, leaving Chanta blinking, underwear inside out, and wishing he\'92d asked to come with anyway.
